# Copy to .env next to compose.yaml (docs/operations/docker.md). Two lines to fill in; everything else has a default.

# The hub image, named after the repository: ghcr.io/<owner>/persona (before 0.1.2 it was ghcr.io/<owner>/persona).
PERSONA_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/persona:latest

# How phones reach the hub: this machine's LAN address with the port below, a VPN address, or an HTTPS reverse proxy
# in front of it. Left empty, the hub says so when it starts and pairing has no address until it is set (here, or on
# the admin page's network section).
PERSONA_PUBLIC_URL=http://nas.example.com:18660

# Who may open the hub's admin UI at <PERSONA_PUBLIC_URL>/admin (adding computers, devices, network; docs/operations/admin.md):
#   loopback  only inside this container (docker compose exec): safest, least handy
#   lan       this machine and private addresses (inside a container the NAS's LAN cannot be told from other private
#             networks, so any private address counts), but never the bridge's gateway: Docker's userland proxy
#             relays from there, the internet included (Docker Desktop and OrbStack relay every browser so). The default
#   cidrs     this machine and the networks in PERSONA_ADMIN_CIDRS (e.g. your VPN's), comma separated
#   any       anyone over the hub's TLS port: only the admin password and its sign-in limits stand in the way
# Set here, it wins over hub.json and the admin page cannot change it. Left unset, the hub says so when it starts
# and uses hub.json's admin.access (lan when that is not set either). Setting the first admin password takes the
# one-time code in `docker compose logs hub` (or `docker compose exec hub personad hub admin setup-code`).
PERSONA_ADMIN_ACCESS=lan
# PERSONA_ADMIN_CIDRS=192.0.2.0/24,100.64.0.0/10

# Optional. Without them a first start makes up what it needs:
# The database's password. Unset: a random one on the first start, kept in data/docker/db-password. Set: letters and
# digits only (it goes into the database URL as it is), e.g. `openssl rand -hex 24`. Postgres takes it only when it
# first creates its data folder: changing it later locks the hub out (change it inside Postgres too,
# ALTER USER persona PASSWORD '...'). Deployments that already have one keep it here. Leaving it unset needs an image
# built with this compose.yaml or later: an older PERSONA_IMAGE tag cannot read the generated one, so set it then.
# PERSONA_DB_PASSWORD=
# The owner of the data folders (`id -u` / `id -g` of the NAS user that should own them). Unset: whoever owns the data
# folder next to compose.yaml (1000 when Docker made it itself).
# PUID=1000
# PGID=1000
# TZ=UTC
# The ports on this machine: the hub's (HTTP) and its TLS port. Keep the TLS one 18662 (that is the port the hub
# tells apps); put an address in front to publish it on one address only, e.g. <NAS LAN address>:18662.
# PERSONA_PORT=18660
# PERSONA_TLS_PORT=18662
# A private image: a token that can read it (GitHub: read:packages), so the hub can see when a newer image is out.
# PERSONA_REGISTRY_TOKEN=
# Docker Hub out of reach: postgres through a mirror or a local registry, e.g. <registry>/library/postgres:18-alpine
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:18-alpine
# Where the hub fetches node builds for computers of other platforms (macOS, the other Linux architecture) and the
# devices' own program (persona-node): a release's dl/ or a mirror of it on your network; off: never (put the builds in
# data/bin/nodes instead). Unset: the image's own release site, if it was built with one. See docs/operations/docker.md "Node
# builds".
# PERSONA_NODE_PACKAGES=https://<mirror>/dl
# 0: the hub and the built-in manager run on personad, the program that runs everything, as older images did, rather
# than as persona-hub and persona-manager (docs/operations/docker.md "Programs"). Unset: their own programs.
# PERSONA_SPLIT_PROGRAMS=0
# Disk writes (docs/operations/docker.md "Disk writes"). Unset: what compose did before, and PostgreSQL's own defaults,
# except wal_compression (auto) and checkpoint_timeout (15min).
# How often each container is checked: every run makes Docker rewrite and fsync the container's state. Longer: fewer
# writes, but a stuck container is noticed later and `docker compose up` waits about one postgres interval more.
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_HEALTHCHECK_INTERVAL=5s
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_HEALTHCHECK_TIMEOUT=5s
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_HEALTHCHECK_RETRIES=30
# PERSONA_HUB_HEALTHCHECK_INTERVAL=30s
# PERSONA_HUB_HEALTHCHECK_TIMEOUT=5s
# PERSONA_HUB_HEALTHCHECK_RETRIES=3
# off: commits return before the WAL is flushed; a crash can lose the last up to 3 x PERSONA_POSTGRES_WAL_WRITER_DELAY
# of commits, but never corrupts the database. These win over ALTER SYSTEM.
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT=on
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_WAL_WRITER_DELAY=200ms
# Longer: fewer checkpoints and full page writes, a longer crash recovery. 5min is PostgreSQL's own default.
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_CHECKPOINT_TIMEOUT=15min
# auto: lz4 (smaller WAL for a little CPU) when the postgres image takes it (15 or later, built with lz4), else off.
# off, pglz, lz4 or zstd: that one.
# PERSONA_POSTGRES_WAL_COMPRESSION=auto
# Log driver for both containers, json-file or local (other drivers reject the size options), and how much each keeps.
# Either replaces Synology's db driver: Container Manager's Log tab then stays empty, `docker compose logs` works.
# PERSONA_LOG_DRIVER=json-file
# PERSONA_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
# PERSONA_LOG_MAX_FILE=3
# A write-sensitive host (btrfs, a single SSD): health checks every 60s (postgres retries 5), synchronous_commit off,
# wal_writer_delay 1s, the local log driver.
